On Jul 14, 10:05 am, "3cnf...@gmail.com" <3cnf...@gmail.com> wrote:

> My playing style has improved because I *plan ahead for mistakes to
> happen*. I no longer place items as important (and light!) as magic
> markers, essential scrolls and wands, or rings in bags of holding, and
> place then instead in an oilskin sack once I find one. My goal, in
> fact, is to treat the bag of holding as a "mobile stash" of extra crap
> I *might need* on the way up/down to my *real* stash. So when my bag
> of holding gets cursed because I forgot to obsessively dip it in holy
> water (stupid monkey!), it's light enough that I can still *hold it*
> and dip it into holy water/cancel it/etc.

I always keep my BoH inside a regular or oilskin sack, assuming I find
one of each (which I almost always do). I keep critical items in main
inventory or inside the sack, and less critical stuff inside the BoH
since it will take more turns to get to. That way the BoH is almost
never in main inventory long enough to get hit by a curse (though I do
always bless it as soon as I have enough holy water). This also helps
prevent accidentally putting a wand of cancellation inside the BoH
(though it is still possible, I think I've only lost a BoH that way
once).

